#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130006} :activity/id #uuid "9d3054a0-685e-5614-91ab-523403e24015" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050753 :topic/id #uuid "63beb30b-812d-5842-a68b-ba473953a810" :topic/math-academy-id 2038} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130369 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ First, we create a table of values.\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nWe do not consider negative values for the input because the square root of a negative number is not a real number.\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/39/39c60b7e997c4af7b823796c0b94144572e7bff1be083564ab9fa29fd66d4a42.png)\n\nSome key points to note:\n\n- The graph passes through the points $(1,1)$ and $(0,0).$\n\n- The domain  is $x\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n- The range  is $y\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n- The function is increasing over its entire domain.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty,$ though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large." :tutorial/id #uuid "ba6d6815-5a19-50e1-ae6a-8182c47cdba8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3789 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "41b7c9a3-4455-508e-8268-491f140549d3" :step/math-academy-id 21125 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129152 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128970 :question/id #uuid "6b031a4c-79e9-51a7-92a9-8bfde77cca3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5868"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d61f93a-1acd-584c-a901-458b3cb1921e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532637}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of the Square Root Function"} :step/id #uuid "93cdc787-ff33-54c3-80e1-2f874e3e7c93" :s